The implementation is complete. Here's a summary of what I did:

## Change

**`crates/burn-jit/src/ops/int_ops.rs`** — the bitwise left shift for integer tensors was casting both operands from the tensor's native element type `I` to `u32` before executing the shift:

```rust
// before
fn bitwise_left_shift(lhs: IntTensor<Self>, rhs: IntTensor<Self>) -> IntTensor<Self> {
    let lhs_cast = kernel::cast::<R, I, u32>(lhs);
    let rhs_cast = kernel::cast::<R, I, u32>(rhs);
    launch_binop_int::<R, u32, kernel::BitwiseShlOp>(lhs_cast, rhs_cast)
}
```

That silent conversion to `u32` truncated the declared element type (e.g. an `i64` tensor would be reduced to 32 bits before the shift). I changed it to run the shift directly in the tensor's native type `I`:

```rust
fn bitwise_left_shift(lhs: IntTensor<Self>, rhs: IntTensor<Self>) -> IntTensor<Self> {
    launch_binop_int::<R, I, kernel::BitwiseShlOp>(lhs, rhs)
}

fn bitwise_left_shift_scalar(lhs: IntTensor<Self>, rhs: IntElem<Self>) -> IntTensor<Self> {
    launch_scalar_binop_int::<R, I, BitwiseShlOp>(lhs, rhs)
}
```

`BitwiseShlOp` is already implemented generically over any `N: Int`, so no other changes were needed. `cargo check -p burn-jit` passes.

I left `bitwise_right_shift` unchanged (it keeps the `u32` path) since the task scoped the fix to the left shift, and per your instruction I did not touch anything under `tests/` — the test skip logic for small integer types (max value `< 512` when cast to `u32`) will come from the verification system's test patch.
